## Computation of $H^*(\mathbb{C}P^k;\mathbb{Z})$ and $H^*(\mathbb{H}P^k;\mathbb{Z})$ via Poincaré Duality

### Additive structure

Both $\mathbb{C}P^k$ and $\mathbb{H}P^k$ admit CW-structures with a single cell in each relevant dimension:

- **$\mathbb{C}P^k$** (real dimension $2k$): one cell in each even dimension $0,2,4,\dots,2k$. Since there are no odd-dimensional cells, every cellular boundary map is zero. Hence
$$H_i(\mathbb{C}P^k;\mathbb{Z}) \cong \begin{cases}\mathbb{Z} & i=0,2,\dots,2k,\\ 0 & \text{otherwise.}\end{cases}$$
All groups are free, so by the UCT, $H^i(\mathbb{C}P^k;\mathbb{Z})\cong \mathbb{Z}$ for $i=0,2,\dots,2k$ and $0$ otherwise.

- **$\mathbb{H}P^k$** (real dimension $4k$): one cell in each dimension $0,4,8,\dots,4k$. Same argument gives
$$H^i(\mathbb{H}P^k;\mathbb{Z})\cong\mathbb{Z}\quad\text{for }i=0,4,\dots,4k,\qquad 0\text{ otherwise.}$$

### Ring structure of $H^*(\mathbb{C}P^k;\mathbb{Z})$

**Claim.** $H^*(\mathbb{C}P^k;\mathbb{Z})\cong \mathbb{Z}[\alpha]/(\alpha^{k+1})$, $|\alpha|=2$.

**Proof by induction on $k$.**

*Base case* $k=1$: $\mathbb{C}P^1\cong S^2$, so $H^*(S^2;\mathbb{Z})=\mathbb{Z}[\alpha]/(\alpha^2)$, $|\alpha|=2$. ✓

*Inductive step.* Assume the result for $\mathbb{C}P^{k-1}$. Let $i:\mathbb{C}P^{k-1}\hookrightarrow\mathbb{C}P^k$ be the standard inclusion (a hyperplane). Since $\mathbb{C}P^k$ is obtained from $\mathbb{C}P^{k-1}$ by attaching a single $2k$-cell (attaching map the Hopf map $S^{2k-1}\to\mathbb{C}P^{k-1}$), the cofiber is
$$\mathbb{C}P^k/\mathbb{C}P^{k-1}\cong S^{2k}.$$
The long exact sequence in cohomology for the pair $(\mathbb{C}P^k,\mathbb{C}P^{k-1})$ yields
$$\cdots\to \widetilde H^{j-1}(S^{2k})\to H^j(\mathbb{C}P^k)\xrightarrow{\,i^*\,}H^j(\mathbb{C}P^{k-1})\to\widetilde H^j(S^{2k})\to\cdots$$
Since $\widetilde H^j(S^{2k})=\mathbb{Z}$ only at $j=2k$ and vanishes otherwise, the restriction
$$i^*:H^j(\mathbb{C}P^k)\xrightarrow{\;\sim\;}H^j(\mathbb{C}P^{k-1})$$
is an **isomorphism for all $j<2k$**.

Choose a generator $\alpha_k\in H^2(\mathbb{C}P^k)$ with $i^*(\alpha_k)=\alpha_{k-1}$ (the generator of $H^2(\mathbb{C}P^{k-1})$); this is possible since $i^*$ is an iso on $H^2$.

**For $1\le j\le k-1$:** By the induction hypothesis, $\alpha_{k-1}^{\,j}$ generates $H^{2j}(\mathbb{C}P^{k-1})$. Since $i^*$ is a ring homomorphism and an isomorphism in degree $2j<2k$,
$$i^*(\alpha_k^{\,j})=\alpha_{k-1}^{\,j}\;\text{generates}\;H^{2j}(\mathbb{C}P^{k-1})\;\Longrightarrow\;\alpha_k^{\,j}\;\text{generates}\;H^{2j}(\mathbb{C}P^k).$$

**For $j=k$ (the key Poincaré duality step):** $\mathbb{C}P^k$ is a closed orientable $2k$-manifold, so Poincaré duality applies. All cohomology groups are free abelian, so the cup-product pairing
$$H^2(\mathbb{C}P^k)\times H^{2k-2}(\mathbb{C}P^k)\xrightarrow{\;\smile\;}H^{2k}(\mathbb{C}P^k)\cong\mathbb{Z}$$
is a **perfect pairing** of free abelian groups. We have established:
- $\alpha_k$ generates $H^2(\mathbb{C}P^k)\cong\mathbb{Z}$,
- $\alpha_k^{k-1}$ generates $H^{2k-2}(\mathbb{C}P^k)\cong\mathbb{Z}$ (case $j=k-1$ above).

Perfectness means the cup product of two generators is a generator:
$$\alpha_k\smile\alpha_k^{k-1}=\alpha_k^{\,k}=\pm\,(\text{generator of }H^{2k}(\mathbb{C}P^k)).$$
Hence $\alpha_k^{\,k}$ generates $H^{2k}(\mathbb{C}P^k)$, in particular $\alpha_k^{\,k}\neq 0$.

Finally $\alpha_k^{\,k+1}=0$ since $H^{2k+2}(\mathbb{C}P^k)=0$ (degree exceeds the manifold dimension). Combining: $\alpha_k^{\,j}$ generates $H^{2j}$ for every $0\le j\le k$, and $\alpha_k^{k+1}=0$, so
$$\boxed{H^*(\mathbb{C}P^k;\mathbb{Z})\cong \mathbb{Z}[\alpha]/(\alpha^{k+1}),\quad |\alpha|=2.}\qquad\square$$

### Ring structure of $H^*(\mathbb{H}P^k;\mathbb{Z})$

**Claim.** $H^*(\mathbb{H}P^k;\mathbb{Z})\cong\mathbb{Z}[\beta]/(\beta^{k+1})$, $|\beta|=4$.

The proof is **verbatim** the same with $4$ in place of $2$.

*Base case* $k=1$: $\mathbb{H}P^1\cong S^4$, so $H^*(S^4;\mathbb{Z})=\mathbb{Z}[\beta]/(\beta^2)$, $|\beta|=4$. ✓

*Inductive step.* $\mathbb{H}P^k$ is obtained from $\mathbb{H}P^{k-1}$ by attaching a $4k$-cell (attaching map the quaternionic Hopf map $S^{4k-1}\to\mathbb{H}P^{k-1}$), so $\mathbb{H}P^k/\mathbb{H}P^{k-1}\cong S^{4k}$. The same long exact sequence argument shows the restriction $i^*:H^j(\mathbb{H}P^k)\to H^j(\mathbb{H}P^{k-1})$ is an isomorphism for all $j<4k$.

Choose $\beta_k\in H^4(\mathbb{H}P^k)$ with $i^*(\beta_k)=\beta_{k-1}$. For $1\le j\le k-1$, the induction hypothesis gives $\beta_{k-1}^{\,j}$ generates $H^{4j}(\mathbb{H}P^{k-1})$, and since $i^*$ is a ring isomorphism in degree $4j<4k$, $\beta_k^{\,j}$ generates $H^{4j}(\mathbb{H}P^k)$.

For $j=k$: $\mathbb{H}P^k$ is a closed orientable $4k$-manifold. Poincaré duality gives the perfect pairing
$$H^4(\mathbb{H}P^k)\times H^{4k-4}(\mathbb{H}P^k)\xrightarrow{\;\smile\;}H^{4k}(\mathbb{H}P^k)\cong\mathbb{Z}.$$
Since $\beta_k$ generates $H^4$ and $\beta_k^{k-1}$ generates $H^{4k-4}$, perfectness yields
$$\beta_k\smile\beta_k^{k-1}=\beta_k^{\,k}=\pm\,(\text{generator of }H^{4k}(\mathbb{H}P^k)),$$
so $\beta_k^{\,k}\neq 0$. Also $\beta_k^{k+1}=0$ since $H^{4k+4}(\mathbb{H}P^k)=0$. Therefore
$$\boxed{H^*(\mathbb{H}P^k;\mathbb{Z})\cong\mathbb{Z}[\beta]/(\beta^{k+1}),\quad |\beta|=4.}\qquad\square$$

### Summary

| Space | Cohomology ring | Generator degree |
|---|---|---|
| $\mathbb{C}P^k$ | $\mathbb{Z}[\alpha]/(\alpha^{k+1})$ | $|\alpha|=2$ |
| $\mathbb{H}P^k$ | $\mathbb{Z}[\beta]/(\beta^{k+1})$ | $|\beta|=4$ |

The role of Poincaré duality is decisive: it is the perfect pairing $H^d\times H^{n-d}\to H^n\cong\mathbb{Z}$ (with $d=2$ or $4$, $n=2k$ or $4k$) that forces the top power $\alpha^k$ (resp. $\beta^k$) to be a generator of $H^{2k}$ (resp. $H^{4k}$), i.e. nonzero — the one fact that the cellular/cofiber induction alone cannot establish. Once $\alpha^k\neq 0$ is known, the cofiber isomorphisms propagate the ring structure down through all lower powers, giving the truncated polynomial algebra.

